it's great to be joined today by Jackie  say--let who is an advocate for the  rights of independent voters also  president of independent voting which is  the largest network of independent  leaders and activists in the US with  organization in 40 states Jackie I think  a good place to start before we get into  third parties is maybe talking a little  bit about independent voters there's  sometimes the idea that by virtue of  being an independent voter you're either  a centrist or in some way neutral  between conservative and political ideas  for the most part that's not true though  right I mean for the most part  independent voters have no party  affiliation but they do still tend to be  on one side or the other of the  political spectrum in my experience  independents span the entire political  spectrum from right to left when you ask  them about social issues or policy  issues those kinds of things  however the bond that ties independents  together and you know as you know this  is now 44 percent of the country who  identify themselves as independents the  bond that ties them together is a belief  that the political system is broken that  the parties serve largely their own  self-preservation and not the the  purpose and the mission of representing  the interests of the American people and  they have declared themselves to be  independents because they want to make a  statement about that  sure and I guess my point is and I'm  curious whether whether you would agree  that an independent party is sometimes  thought of as a definitive centrist  party but that in reality that there's  no rule that that that says that that's  the way it would have to be you have  lots of independents that are on the  right and even to the right of the  Republican Party you have lots of  independents that are certainly to the  left of the Democratic Party and a would  you agree that it would be wrong to  assume that an independent party of any  substantial size would be necessarily  simply a centrist party between  Democrats and Republicans  I would totally agree with that let's  talk a little bit about the sort of  practical paths towards third party  growth and engagement I've pointed out  in the last several years that I think  that there are two major impediments to  growing the number of votes for third  party candidates at least in  presidential elections number one is  campaign finance and sort of how  politics is financed in the United  States and number two it's the lack of a  voting system like an instant runoff  voting system that would allow someone  to more easily support the candidate  that their politics most closely aligned  with with my which might not be a  Republican or independent without  possibly ending up helping the candidate  they most disagree with assess my  assessment of those two items I think  look in the last 25 years or so there  actually have been independent parties  that have exploded and I and I do use  the term exploded purposely onto the  political scene most notably I think the  Reform Party in the 1990s that was the  political party that grew out of what's  known today as the Perot rebellion after  Ross Perot Rose first run in 1992 and  for a period of about five or six years  there was the framework shall we say of  of a third party that was  non-ideological in its orientation that  was reform oriented and its mission and  I brought together people across the  political spectrum across geographical  divides ideological divides  socio-economic divides etc and and had a  brief shining moment if you will as as  as a new not just a new party but a new  kind of political party now for a  variety of reasons the Reform Party was  destroyed and disabled after this  six-year experiment and I was a part of  that and it was you know an  energized and controversial political  effort controversial in a lot of  political circles so I think that when  you when you point to barriers and  surely those barriers are there from  ballot access to the fear of spoiler  voting to campaign finance regulations  to debate regulations to the ways that  elections are administered namely as a  bipartisan rather than a nonpartisan  affair and so on and so forth all of  that comes into play there's no question  about that and I think that part of  what's going on right now in the reform  movement and in the independent  political movement is there's a lot of  energy and a lot of attention being paid  to what kinds of structural reforms are  necessary in order to make it more  possible for a third force to emerge  whether it emerges as a party or not  that's kind of an interesting question  frankly because given that the sentiment  of so many Americans particularly  independence but not exclusively is to  be disappointed and distressed by the  parties and by the kinds of partisanship  that they produce it's not altogether  clear to me that a third party is going  to be or should be the form that a new  force takes  oh yeah well so I think it's interesting  imagining that because you do have  independents who are not Republicans or  Democrats because they don't want to be  part of either of those two parties  there are also independents who are sort  of against the concept of political  parties and those are two different  things and it's important to distinguish  yes and I would add I'm not agree with  you and I think that that's very true  they're not just against the concept of  political parties they're just against  activities corrupt authoritarian  controlling structures that limit  competition and are completely focused  on you know their own political power  over the interests of ordinary people  they're against that and so then the  issue becomes well what are we going to  create that does give  well a platform and tools to fully  participate in what is supposed to be a  self governance process in this country  but which frankly the parties are one of  the major obstacles to realizing that so  Jackie and your sort of context right is  the do you think that the independent  that there's this sort of stereotypical  or carriage and caricatured independent  who says something like well I'm  socially liberal and fiscally  conservative so therefore I'm not a  Republican or a Democrat I'll just vote  for whichever Republican or Democrat is  closest to my views that's not really  sounding to me like the type of  independent that you're talking about or  targeting right why do you say that well  because you seem to be more focused and  not as a criticism but you seem to be  more focused on those who are  independents not because they share  views with some democrats and  republicans but rather because they feel  that the infrastructure that exists  between these two parties and the  corruption that sort of inherent to our  political finance system or the problem  it's not about I'm kind of with the  Democrats on some issues and with  Republicans on others but it's a  different paradigm altogether that's  what I'm gathering tell me if I'm wrong  I see no well that's true I think that's  accurate though i I guess the one caveat  I would add here is that I actually  think that most Americans are socially  liberal broadly defined I agree and  fiscally conservative broadly defined  so don't naturally agree on that part  get talked about in in a somewhat  elevated way or or a categorical kind of  way but I mean you know I think in in  people's personal and private lives and  family lives you know everybody has to  manage a budget and you're only allowed  to borrow but so much you know and most  people have looked kind of  live-and-let-live attitude and a you  know and and somewhere between  libertarian and and you know progressive  if you want to use that kind of language  view of social issues but I think in a  way maybe me  the point that you're making and here I  think it's very important is that the  parties and the structure of the parties  actually prevent people from coming  together across the ideological divide  you know one of the things that in my  experience in Reform Party for example  which was a true left-right coalition  was that we talked a lot about the  building a bridge between the overtaxed  and the underserved for example and and  finding that there was actually a  tremendous commonality of interest in  purpose among many different kinds of  Americans but the party system really  channels people into a kind of divide  and and I think that's part of what  we're grappling with and I think again I  think independents have an important  role to play in helping the country as a  whole  reshape some of these paradigms and  create some new tools for us to deal  with all of this in a different way  Jackie in the limited time we have left  I'd love your brief thoughts on whether  2020 presents an interesting opportunity  for independent voters and possibly for  something other than just a Democrat and  a Republican as a result of the sort of  chaos that the Trump presidency has sort  of sown whether that's a challenger from  within the Republican Party that ends up  running third party or in some other way  do you think that the 2020 election has  some interesting opportunities for  independence I think it does I think it  raises a lot of really important  tactical issues and if you will  developmental issues for the country but  yeah there's a huge amount of chaos and  both parties are in chaos by the way not  just Trump's party but the Democratic  Party is as well and you have this huge  swath of Americans that are out there  that are not aligned with any political  party there's I think there's a basis  for some new and you know and pretty  pretty explosive things Apple we've been  speaking with Jackie say--let she is  president of independent voting and a  super interesting topic that we've been  covering for a long time Jackie I really  appreciate you talking to me today  well thank you so much for inviting me  it was great talking to you too

